<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/logic-arguments">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An argument, in philosophy, is not a quarrel. It is a structure: a set of statements — the
    <strong>premises</strong> — offered as reasons in support of another statement, the
    <strong>conclusion</strong>. The move from premises to conclusion is an <strong>inference</strong>,
    and logic is the study of which inferences actually hold. Seen this way, arguments are everywhere
    and quarrels are the least interesting place they appear. A doctor who orders a test has run an
    argument ("the symptom pattern is consistent with X, the test distinguishes X, therefore test").
    A bridge engineer argues from load tables to girder thickness. A detective argues from fingerprints
    to a suspect. What philosophy adds is a vocabulary for saying precisely <em>why</em> one of these
    structures works and another collapses — a skill roughly as useful as knowing how to read.
  </p>
  <p>
    This matters because arguments hide. Very few real arguments arrive in neat numbered form; they
    arrive wrapped in persuasion, anecdote and emphasis. The first skill of logic is
    <strong>identifying the argument within a text</strong> — finding where the reasoning starts and
    stops — and the second is rebuilding it cleanly so it can be judged. This lesson walks the full
    ladder: what an argument is, the three basic forms it can take, the two independent ways it can
    succeed or fail, the respectable middle-ground forms (analogy and inference to the best
    explanation), the ways arguments cheat, and the technical terms philosophers use to classify
    statements themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>Finding the argument: premises, conclusions, inferences</h2>
  <p>
    To identify an argument, look for two things. First, <strong>indicator words</strong>:
    conclusions are announced by <em>therefore, thus, so, hence, it follows that, consequently</em>;
    premises are announced by <em>because, since, given that, for the reason that</em>. Second, and
    more reliable, ask: <em>what is this passage trying to get me to accept, and what is it offering
    as support?</em> Not every passage argues. A description reports; an explanation tells you
    <em>why</em> something already agreed is true ("the bridge collapsed because the bolts were
    counterfeit" — no conclusion is in dispute); an argument tries to establish that something
    <em>is</em> true. Rhetoric merely aims to make you feel. Mixed passages are normal: an opinion
    column usually contains one argument and three paragraphs of decoration. The logician's job is
    extraction.
  </p>
  <p>
    Extraction ends in <strong>standard form</strong>: list the premises, then the conclusion, with
    each premise a single self-contained claim. Hidden premises — the ones everyone is assumed to
    know ("Tuesday, therefore the market is open" assumes <em>the market opens on Tuesdays</em>) —
    should be written in explicitly, because they are where arguments usually fail. The finished
    reconstruction can then be tested mechanically, which is what the widget below does for the most
    famous argument in history.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the classic syllogism, assembled step by step" widget="equationStep" config={sylCfg} />

  <h2>Three forms of argument: deduction, induction, abduction</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Deduction</strong> trades in certainty. A deductive argument claims that
    <em>if</em> the premises are true, the conclusion <em>must</em> be true — the conclusion is
    already contained in the premises, the way a verdict is contained in a complete set of evidence.
    All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is mortal. If you accept the
    premises, rejecting the conclusion is not permitted. Mathematics and formal logic live here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Induction</strong> trades in probability. It generalises from observed cases to
    unobserved ones: every swan I have seen is white; therefore all swans are white. The premises can
    be true and the conclusion false — Europe was certain about those swans until 1697, when Dutch
    sailors found black ones in Australia. That is not a defect to be fixed but the price of the
    ticket: induction is the only path to knowledge of the unobserved, and all of empirical science
    runs on it. An inductive argument is judged <em>strong</em> or <em>weak</em> (never valid or
    invalid), and strength turns on sample size, representativeness and how cautiously the
    generalisation is stated.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Abduction</strong> — inference to the best explanation — reasons backwards. You see wet
    grass; you infer rain, because rain would explain the grass and no competing explanation does as
    well. Sherlock Holmes, despite his catchphrase, almost never deduces; he abduces. Medicine,
    detective work, debugging and the historical sciences (evolution, cosmology) are abductive at
    their core: we cannot watch the past or the microscopic, so we ask which hypothesis would best
    explain what we <em>can</em> see. The judgement of "best" weighs explanatory power, fit with
    background knowledge, and simplicity — which brings us to a famous razor.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "deductive" does not mean "good" and "inductive" does not mean
  "weaker-willed". They answer different questions. A deduction guarantees its conclusion only
  relative to its premises; an induction openly claims only likelihood. Judged by its own standard,
  a careful induction can be far more trustworthy than a deduction built on false premises.</p>

  <h2>Validity and soundness: two verdicts, both needed</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the distinction that does the most work in philosophy. An argument is
    <strong>valid</strong> if the conclusion follows from the premises <em>if they were true</em> —
    that is, if it is impossible for all the premises to be true while the conclusion is false.
    Validity is about the argument's <em>shape</em> only. An argument is <strong>sound</strong> if it
    is valid <em>and</em> its premises really are true. Soundness is about shape <em>and</em> truth.
    The upshot is that there are exactly two ways an argument can fail: a false premise, or a leaking
    shape — and the two failures are diagnosed by entirely different methods.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example of each failure. Shape-failure: "All fish swim; whales swim; therefore whales
    are fish." Both premises are true, the conclusion false, so the shape leaks — the middle term
    (swimming) never connected whales to the class fish. Truth-failure: "All birds can fly; penguins
    are birds; therefore penguins can fly." The shape here is perfectly valid — yet the conclusion is
    false, because premise one is false (penguins broke it). The standard test for validity is the
    <strong>counterexample method</strong>: keep the shape, swap the words for others, and see whether
    you can make all premises true with a false conclusion. "All cats are animals; dogs are animals;
    therefore dogs are cats" shows the whales-fish shape is invalid once and for all.
  </p>

  <h2>Analogy, hypothetical reasoning, and Ockham's Razor</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Arguments from analogy</strong> claim that because two things are alike in some respects,
    they are alike in the respect at issue. Case law is built on them (this case resembles that
    precedent); so is much moral reasoning (a foetus responds to sound, as a newborn does; therefore
    it deserves similar protection). Analogy is a form of induction, and its strength is honest
    variable work: what matters is whether the <em>shared</em> properties are relevant to the
    <em>target</em> property. Two calculators made by the same factory will likely have the same
    failure rate; two books from the same publisher will not. "Same factory" is relevant to
    manufacturing defects; "same publisher" is not. Most bad analogies fail on relevance, not on
    number of similarities.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Hypothetical reasoning</strong> works with conditionals — <em>if P then Q</em>. Two moves
    with conditionals are always valid: <strong>modus ponens</strong> (P holds, therefore Q) and
    <strong>modus tollens</strong> (Q is false, therefore P is false — "if it rained, the street is
    wet; the street is dry; so it did not rain"). Modus tollens is how hypotheses die: derive a testable
    consequence, show it false, and the hypothesis goes with it. And when several hypotheses survive,
    the tie-breaker is <strong>Ockham's Razor</strong>: do not multiply entities beyond necessity —
    prefer the explanation that posits fewer new kinds of thing. It is a preference, not a proof:
    nature is sometimes baroque. But as a research heuristic it has an excellent record, and it is
    what makes abduction disciplined rather than fantasy. (The razor is named for William of Ockham,
    the 14th-century Franciscan, though the principle is older than he is.)
  </p>

  <h2>How arguments cheat: fallacies</h2>
  <p>
    Some failures are so common they have names. <strong>Circularity / begging the question</strong>:
    the conclusion appears as a premise — "the Bible is true because it says so, and what it says is
    true." The argument has a valid shape but cannot persuade anyone who does not already accept the
    conclusion; it assumes precisely what is in dispute. <strong>Contradiction</strong>: a set of
    premises that denies itself ("P and not-P") entails <em>anything at all</em> in classical logic, so
    an inconsistent position proves nothing. <strong>Affirming the consequent</strong>: "If rain, wet
    ground; the ground is wet; therefore it rained" — invalid, because a sprinkler explains it too.
    <strong>Denying the antecedent</strong>: "If rain, wet ground; no rain; therefore dry ground" —
    same sprinkler refutes it. These four are the formal fallacies: failures of shape.
  </p>
  <p>
    Informal fallacies fail on content. <strong>Ad hominem</strong> attacks the arguer instead of the
    argument — a liar can still state truths. <strong>Straw man</strong> refutes a claim nobody made,
    substituting a weaker version. <strong>False dilemma</strong> offers two options where more exist.
    <strong>Appeal to authority</strong> treats expertise as proof; it lends weight, not certainty,
    and only within the expert's field. <strong>Slippery slope</strong> assumes a chain of
    consequences without defending each link. The study of fallacies is not a debating rifle —
    real arguments are usually imperfect in ordinary ways — but a diagnostic manual for the specific
    diseases that reasoning most often catches.
  </p>

  <h2>The philosopher's adjectives: analytic, a priori, necessary</h2>
  <p>
    Three distinctions classify statements, not arguments, and students mix them up constantly
    because they are independent of one another. <strong>Analytic</strong> statements are true in
    virtue of the meanings of their words: "all bachelors are unmarried" — deny it and you have said
    something confused, not something false. <strong>Synthetic</strong> statements are true in virtue
    of how the world is: "bachelors tend to die younger" needs checking. <strong>A priori</strong>
    knowledge is knowable without experience (arithmetic, pure logic); <strong>a posteriori</strong>
    knowledge requires observation (chemistry, history). <strong>Necessary</strong> truths could not
    have been false in any possible world (2 + 2 = 4; water is H<sub>2</sub>O, given what water is);
    <strong>contingent</strong> truths happen to hold but might not (there being seven continents;
    your current reading of this sentence). The default pairings — analytic with a priori with
    necessary, synthetic with a posteriori with contingent — mostly hold, though philosophers from
    Kant ("synthetic a priori") to Kripke (necessary a posteriori: the H<sub>2</sub>O case) built
    careers on the interesting exceptions. The distinctions matter in practice because they tell you
    <em>how</em> a claim can be settled: by analysis, by observation, or by considering other possible
    worlds.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Logic's history is the story of structure becoming machine. Aristotle catalogued valid syllogistic
    forms around 350 BCE; George Boole reduced the syllogism to algebra in 1854 (<em>The Laws of
    Thought</em>); and in 1937 Claude Shannon showed that the same algebra describes electrical
    switching circuits — the digital computer fell out of a philosophy problem. Every
    <code>if</code> statement in every program is modus ponens wearing a compiler. Twentieth-century
    logic pushed further: Frege and Russell rebuilt arithmetic inside logic; Gödel (1931) proved that
    any consistent formal system rich enough for arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove —
    a limit result that changed philosophy's self-understanding. On the ground floor, though, the
    craft is unchanged since Athens: extract the argument, put it in standard form, ask whether the
    shape holds (validity) and whether the inputs are true (soundness), and treat every shortcut
    around those questions as a suspect. The person who can do that reliably is very hard to fool —
    including by their own arguments, which is the harder and more valuable half of the skill.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Aristotle, Prior Analytics (c. 350 BCE); William of Ockham (c. 1323); Boole, The Laws of Thought (1854); Shannon, "A Symbolic Analysis of Relay and Switching Circuits" (1937); Gödel (1931); Kripke, Naming and Necessity (1972).</p>
